  "modules": [
    { "name": "Error", "source": "Src/Error.rux", "header": "Why a time zone could not be had." },
    {
      "name": "Rule",
      "source": "Src/Rule.rux",
      "header": "The POSIX `TZ` rule: a standard time, perhaps a daylight time, and the dates each starts on.\nA rule such as `CET-1CEST,M3.5.0,M10.5.0/3` says how a zone keeps time in every year, which is how a TZif file\ndescribes the years after its last listed change, and how the `TZ` variable may describe a zone without any file.\nOffsets in a rule are written west of Greenwich positive, the opposite of every other offset, so they are negated\nas they are read. A change takes effect at its time in the local time it ends: the start of daylight time in\nstandard time and its end in daylight time."
    },
    {
      "name": "System",
      "source": "Src/System.rux",
      "header": "The zones the system knows: those in its time-zone directory, and the one it is set to.\nA zone is loaded by its tz database name, such as `America/New_York`, from the directory `TZDIR` names, or else from\nthe directories the Unix systems keep the database in. Windows keeps no such directory, so there a zone loads only\nfrom a `TZDIR` a program provides. The local zone is the one `TZ` names, or `/etc/localtime`, on the Unix systems,\nand on Windows the zone the system is set to, as rules for its standard and daylight time."
    },
    {
      "name": "Zone",
      "source": "Src/Zone.rux",
      "header": "A time zone: the offsets it has kept, the moments they changed, and the rule it keeps after the last change.\nThe history comes from a TZif file, RFC 8536, as the tz database distributes it; the rule from the file's footer or\nfrom a POSIX `TZ` string. Asking what local time a moment is takes a binary search of the changes, or the rule after\nthem. Asking the reverse, which moment a local time is, can have two answers in the hour clocks fall back and none\nin the hour they spring forward, so `Resolve` says which happened and gives both candidate moments."
    }
  ],
